WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.520 position:50% size:92% align:center
The starter’s two sixteen-by-sixteen input

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.441 position:50% size:92% align:center
matrices contain ones. One warp cooperates

00:00:06.441 --> 00:00:08.109 position:50% size:92% align:center
on the matrix operation.

00:00:08.329 --> 00:00:11.194 position:50% size:92% align:center
Follow the data through that cooperation instead

00:00:11.194 --> 00:00:12.985 position:50% size:92% align:center
of assigning an output element

00:00:12.985 --> 00:00:14.239 position:50% size:92% align:center
to a particular lane.

00:00:16.259 --> 00:00:19.075 position:50% size:92% align:center
A fragment is the matrix API’s container for

00:00:19.075 --> 00:00:21.251 position:50% size:92% align:center
part of the collective operation’s

00:00:21.251 --> 00:00:22.147 position:50% size:92% align:center
working state.

00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:24.597 position:50% size:92% align:center
The fragment layout across lanes is

00:00:24.597 --> 00:00:27.591 position:50% size:92% align:center
implementation-dependent. The diagram therefore

00:00:27.591 --> 00:00:30.395 position:50% size:92% align:center
groups fragments by role, not by an invented

00:00:30.395 --> 00:00:31.924 position:50% size:92% align:center
lane-to-element mapping.

00:00:33.944 --> 00:00:37.212 position:50% size:92% align:center
The warp-level matrix call uses the SM’s matrix

00:00:37.212 --> 00:00:39.646 position:50% size:92% align:center
execution capability with the input

00:00:39.646 --> 00:00:41.453 position:50% size:92% align:center
fragments and accumulator.

00:00:41.673 --> 00:00:44.769 position:50% size:92% align:center
It is a collective call. The API operation is

00:00:44.769 --> 00:00:47.659 position:50% size:92% align:center
not a promise about one particular machine

00:00:47.659 --> 00:00:50.548 position:50% size:92% align:center
instruction or a fixed execution duration.

00:00:52.568 --> 00:00:55.517 position:50% size:92% align:center
For C at row zero and column zero, sixteen

00:00:55.517 --> 00:00:58.748 position:50% size:92% align:center
products of one contribute sixteen point zero.

00:00:58.748 --> 00:01:00.995 position:50% size:92% align:center
The initial accumulator is zero.

00:01:01.215 --> 00:01:03.620 position:50% size:92% align:center
The final store writes the collective

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.311 position:50% size:92% align:center
result back to GPU memory.

00:01:07.331 --> 00:01:10.362 position:50% size:92% align:center
Now consider startingValue equal to five point

00:01:10.362 --> 00:01:13.261 position:50% size:92% align:center
zero, with the same input matrices and warp.

00:01:13.481 --> 00:01:16.515 position:50% size:92% align:center
Pause and predict C at row zero, column zero.

00:01:16.515 --> 00:01:19.414 position:50% size:92% align:center
Does changing that starting value require a

00:01:19.414 --> 00:01:21.908 position:50% size:92% align:center
different set of cooperating threads?

00:01:27.728 --> 00:01:30.588 position:50% size:92% align:center
The selected output becomes twenty-one point

00:01:30.588 --> 00:01:33.579 position:50% size:92% align:center
zero: five plus the sixteen products. The same

00:01:33.579 --> 00:01:35.984 position:50% size:92% align:center
warp still participates collectively.

00:01:36.204 --> 00:01:39.547 position:50% size:92% align:center
Fragments supply operands and working state; the

00:01:39.547 --> 00:01:42.750 position:50% size:92% align:center
SM supplies shared matrix hardware; GPU memory

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.631 position:50% size:92% align:center
receives the stored result.
